            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.254,
            0.083,
            0.883,
            0.141
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The unique shape, size, and structure of cells help them carry out their specific functions. But how do these cells help in performing various functions in the body? Let us find out."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.254,
            0.141,
            0.884,
            0.291
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "You observed in Activity 2.3 that inner cheek cells are thin and flat. They form a protective lining on the inner surface of the cheek. Nerve cells also known as "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "neurons",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " carry messages in our body. The elongated shape and branched structure help them reach different parts of the body and pass on messages quickly. Similarly, plant cells also show variation. In plants, too, cells may be rectangular, elongated, oval, or even tube-like. Some plant cells form long tubes that help carry water throughout the plant."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.052,
            0.352,
            0.128,
            0.443
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 3,
              "bbox": [
                0.052,
                0.352,
                0.128,
                0.443
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_6_image_3.jpg",
              "content": ""
            },
            {
              "type": "image_footnote",
              "index": 4,
              "bbox": [
                0.056,
                0.446,
                0.119,
                0.465
              ],
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "(a) Cell"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.254,
            0.29,
            0.885,
            0.478
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "You have already studied the digestive system in Grade 7. Different parts of the digestive system are made up of different types of cells. A group of muscle cells are present in the food pipe. These cells contract and relax in a wave-like manner, pushing the food down to the stomach. This movement is possible because muscle cells are thin, flexible, and spindle-shaped. The stomach also has different types of cells for performing different functions. Muscle cells in the stomach wall help churn the food. Other cells in the inner lining of the stomach produce digestive juices and acid that help break down the food. All these cells work together to make digestion possible."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.161,
            0.445,
            0.233,
            0.565
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 6,
              "bbox": [
                0.161,
                0.445,
                0.233,
                0.565
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_6_image_6.jpg",
              "content": ""
            },
            {
              "type": "image_footnote",
              "index": 8,
              "bbox": [
                0.16,
                0.569,
                0.241,
                0.586
              ],
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "(b) Tissue"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.268,
            0.49,
            0.849,
            0.545
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "2.2 What Are the Levels of Organisation in the Body of a Living Organism?"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.254,
            0.559,
            0.884,
            0.712
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The body of a living organism is organised in a complex way. Cell (Fig. 2.7a), is the basic unit of life, just like a brick is the basic unit of wall (Fig. 2.3d). A group of similar cells forms a type of "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "tissue",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " (Fig. 2.7b). Different tissues are organised to form an "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "organ",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " (Fig. 2.7c). Several organs work together to form an "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "organ system",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " that performs a major function of the body (Fig. 2.7d). All the organ systems together make up a complete "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "organism",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " (Fig. 2.7e) — like a plant or an animal. So, the levels of organisations are:"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.026,
            0.623,
            0.242,
            0.859
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 10,
              "bbox": [
                0.026,
                0.623,
                0.242,
                0.859
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_6_image_10.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.284,
            0.712,
            0.855,
            0.733
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Cell Tissue Organ Organ system Organism",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.16,
            0.771,
            0.242,
            0.805
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(d) Organ system"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.253,
            0.735,
            0.883,
            0.793
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "These levels of organisation help us understand how simple building blocks like cells come together to form a complex living being."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.03,
            0.865,
            0.214,
            0.919
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(e) Organism Fig. 2.7: Levels of organisation of life"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
            0.254,
            0.793,
            0.885,
            0.907
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The life of complex living organisms begins with a single cell— ‘egg’. The egg of any organism has an amazing ability to divide repeatedly to form a complete living being made up of many cells. Such living beings are called "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "multicellular organisms",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ". Animals, including humans, and plants are all examples of multicellular organisms."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
